{{also|toten}}
==German==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|goh|de}} {{term|tōden|lang=goh}}, compare Old Norse {{term|deyða|lang=non}} (> Danish {{term|døde|lang=da}}, Swedish {{term|döda|lang=sv}}), Gothic {{term|𐌳𐌰𐌿𐌸𐌾𐌰𐌽|tr=dauþjan|lang=got}} and {{etyl|nl|-}} {{term|doden|lang=nl}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|[ˈtøːtn̩]|[ˈtøːtən]|lang=de}}
* {{audio|De-töten.ogg|audio}}

===Verb===
{{de-verb-weak|tötet|tötete|getötet}}

# to [[kill]]
#: ''Zehn Menschen wurden bei dem Anschlag '''getötet'''.''
#: Ten people were '''killed''' in the attack.

====Usage notes====
In formal usage, ''töten'' is an adequate translation of English "to kill" in its exact meaning. Colloquially, the verb {{term|umbringen|lang=de}} is preferred and ''töten'' is rare. In particular, only ''umbringen'' is generally used in figurative meaning. For example: ''Ich bring meinen Freund um, wenn er meine Blumen nicht gegossen hat!'' – I'll kill my boyfriend if he hasn't watered my flowers!

